WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.560
Film Courage: What is the shortest&nbsp;
amount of time you’ve written a script?

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:09.360
Shane Stanley, Filmmaker/Author/Instructor:&nbsp; I&nbsp;
did a 36-hour fade-in-to-the-end for Emmett/Furla&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:19.200
films project called BAD NEWS that I wrote&nbsp;
for them. I did it on a Tuesday, Wednesday,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:30.240
Thursday without sleep, no drugs, without sleep.&nbsp;
I crashed some point at. I physically collapsed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:36.160
in my condo and I woke up at some point&nbsp;
Friday, printed it out, called Emmett/Furla,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:41.040
drove it to them at the Warner lot and they&nbsp;
optioned it. They called me Monday said “We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:46.880
loved it, it’s exactly what we need. We&nbsp;
want it.” That was three days, non-stop.

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:48.560
Film Courage:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:53.200
Did the lack of sleep help you get into a&nbsp;
mindset? Was it some type of sci-fi fantasy?

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:58.720
Shane: No, it was an action film. I knew the&nbsp;
story I wanted to tell and Emmett and Furla&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:04.560
were friends. We’d done a film or two with&nbsp;
them and they were buying. They were working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:08.240
with people at the time that I’d worked with&nbsp;
in the past. They were working with Avi Lerner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:14.400
and Cassian Elwes and Mark Rifkin and some of&nbsp;
those guys from the late 90’s I had worked with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:18.560
who were cranking out these 10 to 20 million&nbsp;
dollar action movies. So I knew what they wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:26.960
and they were paying to do it and these guys&nbsp;
I pitched George on it over the phone and he’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:30.160
like “That sounds cool, get me the script.”&nbsp;
This was like on a Tuesday and I hung up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:32.400
the phone. It was like three o’clock in&nbsp;
the afternoon. I just started writing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:40.080
I started writing. Then I collapsed at some&nbsp;
point Thursday and realized that I wrote The End.

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:44.320
Film Courage: Did you wake up and the room was&nbsp;
spinning? What happened? Did someone find you?

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.320
Shane: I was living alone at the time. It was&nbsp;
after my first divorce. I was back living alone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:56.880
You know us bachelors when we find our&nbsp;
way back to bachelorhood was usually live.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:05.520
It was crazy. I remember that time very vividly&nbsp;
and I remember writing it Tuesday afternoon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:10.080
collapsing some point Thursday and you know&nbsp;
when you wake up Friday everything is still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:13.680
a blur and I just remember hitting print&nbsp;
- there was 96 pages, I just hit print.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:19.360
I punch-holed them, put a brad in, call their&nbsp;
office to make sure Dal was their guy at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:23.440
time who was head of development. I said “Dal it’s&nbsp;
Shane, I’m coming, will you give me a pass?” He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:27.600
goes “Yeah, yeah, yeah. We’re waiting for you,&nbsp;
dude.” Drove onto the Warner lot, have it to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:34.400
Dal and said “Enjoy it.” George called me and said&nbsp;
“Yeah dude, you’ve got a deal.” I will say George&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:40.400
has better taste than that. This was 25 years ago&nbsp;
but yes, that was the fastest script I ever wrote.

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:43.680
Film Courage: Wow…a lot of coffee?

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:47.360
Shane: Oh yeah. Crown and&nbsp;
coffee. I don’t know why.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:54.160
Crown Royal and coffee. I don’t know how…and&nbsp;
an empty stomach. You’ve got to stay awake.

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:56.000
Film Courage: In the little&nbsp;
purple bag…Crown Royal.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:59.280
Shane: I probably smoked a lot too. I went&nbsp;
through a phase where I was smoking a lot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:05.040
I think I probably went through 19 packs&nbsp;
of cigarettes that week. Good times…thank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:10.320
goodness I’m vegan now. I’m making up trying&nbsp;
to take care of myself now that I’m old.

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:15.840
Film Courage: That’s just&nbsp;
quite an image. I can see it.

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:17.840
Shane: I can tell you another story similar.

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:18.784
Film Courage: Please do.

00:03:18.784 --> 00:03:24.240
Shane: I’ll tell you another story similar. We&nbsp;
did NO CODE OF CONDUCT for Dimension which was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:29.520
Miramax. It was a 12 million dollar film we shot.&nbsp;
We had a green light on the script. Charlie Sheen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:36.000
starred in it and we had written it, they didn’t&nbsp;
want to tell that story so they brought two other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:41.280
writers on. A couple of great guys, Ed Masterson&nbsp;
and Bill Gucwa, really cool guys. We ended up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:45.440
becoming very good friends for a long time.&nbsp;
Actually one of them bought a car from me and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:52.480
we got to Phoenix and the director and Charlie&nbsp;
read the script and they go “What the heck is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:56.880
this?” And I said I don’t know, that’s what they&nbsp;
sent. And they go Well we start shooting in 48&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:05.520
hours, get your laptop, fade in. So we rewrote&nbsp;
the script in Arizona two days before we shot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:11.440
from page one and just about a page one&nbsp;
rewrite. That was another all same time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:15.120
in my life. It was in give or take a few&nbsp;
years so I was still able to do that.

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:18.480
Film Courage: And how does this&nbsp;
work with the original credit?

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:24.640
Shane: Who cares! We’re just a bunch of dumb&nbsp;
people making movies. I mean they paid us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:29.040
we were producing the film, they paid the writers.&nbsp;
It was like they had a deal with the writers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:34.480
anyway. So it was like we wrote the film, Charlie,&nbsp;
Brett Michaels and I wrote the script and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:39.680
they agreed to make the movie, we went to Phoenix&nbsp;
to make the movie and then somebody go in the mix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:43.440
and said that’s not the script we want to make and&nbsp;
hired two writers, didn’t tell us anything about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:48.800
it and just sent us a script. The guys never came&nbsp;
out to Arizona. They were just two guys in LA that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:53.600
were told to rewrite a script that they weren’t&nbsp;
happy with because it was originally written for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:58.960
LA and then they send us to Phoenix for a&nbsp;
30 percent tax credit. So we get there and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:02.560
it’s just a domino effect. You change the script&nbsp;
and it’s what the heck? This isn’t what we…Charlie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:06.080
is like this isn’t why I’m here. We didn’t agree&nbsp;
to do this and it wasn’t our company. Charlie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:10.640
Brett and I had a company. They said&nbsp;
come on dynamo, get some Jolt cola or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:18.240
get some coffee and fire it up. Let’s go.&nbsp;
We did a 48 hour, two days before we wrote.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:20.560
We rewrote the whole script and&nbsp;
I don’t think they ever knew it.

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:25.120
Film Courage: So what was hour 47 like?

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:32.640
Shane: Hour 47 I remember being in the Homestead&nbsp;
Village, they hadn’t set us up in our homes yet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:36.480
They gave us homes. It was a 12 million dollar&nbsp;
film in Phoenix in the 90’s. That’s a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:42.400
money. They had us in the Homestead Village and&nbsp;
we each had our own unit and I remember being in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:50.800
Brett’s at his cabinet. I probably smoked three&nbsp;
packs of cigarettes a day and just drank coffee.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:56.240
It was nuts. And then there was a Kenny&nbsp;
Rogers Roasters right at the end. It was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.240 --> 00:06:00.880
like that episode of Seinfeld and I was just&nbsp;
like living on Kenny Rogers Roasters for like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:04.880
two weeks while we were there and those last 48&nbsp;
hours went through like two buckets of chicken.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:09.840
It was brutal, it was brutal. It smelled&nbsp;
in there. And then I had a day and a half&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:14.800
and I remember Joe Lando from Dr. Quinn Medicine&nbsp;
Woman was co-starring in it. He was our bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:20.320
guy and he’s a dear friend (he still is) and I&nbsp;
remember going to my room and collapsing and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:26.960
know the sound of a hotel phone ringing (that loud&nbsp;
chirp). It woke me up about 18 hours later and if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:30.960
you’ve ever woken up from a sleep so deep that you&nbsp;
feel like you’ve gotten punched by Mike Tyson and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:38.000
you’re incoherent, I remember the phone&nbsp;
ringing, waking up and literally I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:41.680
couldn’t not see. Me eyes were open and I was&nbsp;
like Mike Tyson on his hands and knees when

